==English==
[[File:Weibliche brust en.jpg|right|thumb|A woman's breasts, with the areola indicated.]]
[[File:Cactaceae-Cactus in Thailand by Trisorn Triboon 6.JPG|thumb|Cactus '''areolae''' are the little patches from which the spines and [[glochid]]s emerge]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|āreola||small vacant space, garden}}, diminutive of {{m|la|ārea}}. {{doublet|en|areole}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈɹi.ə.lə/|/ˌæ.ɹiˈəʊ.lə/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-areola.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈɹi.ə.lə/|/ˌɛɹ.iˈoʊ.lə/|/ˌæɹ.iˈoʊ.lə/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|iələ|əʊlə|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|areolae|areolæ}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The circular, darkly pigmented area surrounding a [[nipple]]; the [[areola mammae]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Albert Sun|title=From Volunteers, a DNA Database|work=The New York Times|date=2014-04-28|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/04/29/science/from-volunteers-a-dna-database.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Working with the Personal Genome Project, she has collected measurements and images of the '''areolas''' of 150 people so far. One initial finding is that the diameter of the '''areola''' seems unrelated to the number of areola glands — the small bumps in the skin surrounding the nipple.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|anatomy}} Any small circular area that is different from its immediate environment, such as the colored ring around the [[pupil]] of the [[eye]] ([[iris]]) or an inflamed region surrounding a [[pimple]].
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} Any of the small spaces throughout [[w:loose connective tissue|areolar connective tissue]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1847|title=The Medico-Chirurgical Review|volume=51|page=329
|passage=The tubes or elongated spaces of which we have spoken, are not distended with any fluid, but are merely moistened in the same way as the '''areolas''' of ordinary areolar tissue.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} Any of the small spaces between fibres of the tissues of certain lichens.
# {{lb|en|botany}} Small patches, bearing the [[spine]]s and [[glochid]]s [[characteristic]] of the stems of [[cacti]].
#* '''1876''' Richard E. Kunzé  ''Cereus bonplandii'' (Parmet). Documents of the Assembly of the State of New York p.129
#*: Every [[pulvillus]] or '''areola''' is studded with from six to eight spines...  From these pulvilli, also called spiniferous '''areola''', or very close to it, burst the young bud or flower. If the latter, it is then called the [[floriferous]] '''areola''', and the point where the epidermis bursts is of a deep pink tinge.

====Usage notes====
* Not to be confused with {{m|en|aureola}}.

